248: \]
249: Here \(n\) is the \(R\)-exponent after rewriting the original
250: relative class in \(L,R\). This calculation uses the actual Hopf
251: quotient before the flop; it makes no corresponding quotient claim
252: after the fixed curve has become two isolated points.
253: 
254: **Proof: the sign of the central sphere by an oriented plumbing.**
255: Let \(\Sigma_f\) denote \(D^4_s\times S^3_t\) glued to
256: \(S^3_s\times D^4_t\), right coordinate equal to \(f\) of the left
257: coordinate, with the orientations from
258: \(\partial(D^4_s\times D^4_t)\). Take three products
259: \(D_i=D^4_s\times D^4_t\), with orientations \(+,-,+\).
260: Glue their faces by
261: \[
262:  (s_1,t_1)\longmapsto(s_2,t_2)
263:        =(s_1,s_1t_1s_1^{-1}),\qquad
264:  (s_2,t_2)\longmapsto(s_3,t_3)
265:        =(t_2^{-1}s_2t_2,t_2).
266: \]
267: The first is on the \(S^3_s\times D^4_t\) faces, the second on the
268: \(D^4_s\times S^3_t\) faces. Their coordinate orientations agree,
269: while the adjacent eight-ball signs differ, as required for oriented
270: gluing. After rounding, the two remaining faces have boundary word
271: \({\cal A}^{-1}{\cal B}\). The map \({\cal A}\) extends the left
272: face and \({\cal B}^{-1}\) the right face, so the oriented boundary is
273: \[
274:  \Sigma_{{\cal B}^{-1}{\cal A}^{-1}{\cal B}{\cal A}}
275:                        =\Sigma_{{\cal C}^{-1}}.
276: \]
277: 
278: Orient the first zero-section sphere by \(ds\) in \(D_1\) and
279: \(-ds\) in \(D_2\), and the second by \(dt\) in \(D_2\) and
280: \(-dt\) in \(D_3\). At their unique intersection in \(D_2\) their
281: concatenated orientation is \(-ds\,dt\), the ambient orientation.
282: Each normal clutch fixes a real line. Thus
283: \[
284:  Q=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad \sigma(W)=0.
285: \]
286: The normal clutches are \(\operatorname{Ad}_s\) and
287: \(\operatorname{Ad}_{t^{-1}}\). Their normal orientations on their
288: two hemispheres are respectively \(dt\) and \(-ds\).
289: Quaternionic conjugation reverses the four-plane orientation and
290: commutes with every adjoint action, so this second normal-frame
291: orientation introduces no extra Pontryagin sign. The two
292: Pontryagin evaluations are \((p,-p)\), with \(|p|=4\).
293: The eight-manifold has only two four-handles besides its zero-handle;
294: in particular it is spin. Its unimodular intersection form gives
295: \[
296:  p_1^2=(p,-p)Q^{-1}(p,-p)^{\mathsf T}=-32.
297: \]
298: The spin Eells--Kuiper formula consequently gives
299: \[
300:  \mu(\Sigma_{{\cal C}^{-1}})=-\frac1{28},\qquad
301:  \mu(\Sigma_{\cal C})=+\frac1{28}.                   \tag{K23.11}
302: \]
303: 
304: There are two explicit reversals in comparing this plumbing with
305: P3F.22. Its positive Hopf sphere \(u\) has orientation \(-q\),
306: by (K23.7), and its ordered factors are \((u,y)\), whereas the
307: plumbing factors are \((y,q)\). Thus its pieces identify as
308: \[
309:  D^4_u\times S^3_y=-(S^3_y\times D^4_q),\qquad
310:  S^3_u\times D^4_y=-(D^4_y\times S^3_q).
311: \]
312: The pieces exchange and the ambient orientation reverses. The
313: literal gluing map is inverted, giving
314: \(\lambda(F)=-\Sigma_{F^{-1}}\). For a disc-supported class,
315: putting its supporting disc in the common boundary and cancelling
316: the opposite product collars gives
317: \(\Sigma_{F^{-1}}=-\Sigma_F\). Therefore
318: \(\lambda({\cal C})=\Sigma_{\cal C}\), with the positive sign
319: in (K23.11) in the run's convention.
320: 
321: Finally \(L\) and \(R\) extend the seven-disc, so the central exponent
322: of the original relative class is its actual class \(A\bmod28\).
323: Equation (K23.6) adds \(n\) to that exponent, and (K23.10) gives
324: \(n=-6A\). This proves (K23.1) and the displayed examples.
325: 
